# zero_crossing_sweep — the demo that makes the case for phasor_pair.
#
# A sustained tone whose pitch walks continuously from +SWEEP Hz through
# zero down to -SWEEP Hz and back, via single-sideband heterodyne. At
# the moment the modulator rate equals -carrier, the audible frequency
# is exactly 0 Hz — the tone vanishes. As the sweep continues past zero,
# the rotation has reversed direction; the audible pitch climbs back up
# from silence (because humans hear |frequency|), but the sound is now
# the analytic signal rotating the OTHER way around the unit circle.
#
# This continuous transit through zero frequency is mathematically what
# happens when you rotate a complex pair at a varying rate. No
# oscillator can do this — oscillators don't have negative frequencies,
# and pitch shifters introduce grain artifacts at the slow end. Pure
# phasor_pair × cmul produces a glassy, artifact-free glide that
# disappears into silence and re-emerges at a new pitch.
#
# Two voices side by side so the contrast is audible:
#  - REFERENCE (left mix): static 440 Hz carrier, the "before"
#  - SWEEPER (right mix):  same carrier, heterodyne-shifted by a slow LFO
#
# ============================================================ KNOBS
#   K1 (CC 74) — SWEEP DEPTH    ±Hz of the modulator's range (0 → 2000)
#   K2 (CC 71) — SWEEP RATE     LFO speed (1 cycle per 30s → per 3s)
#   K3 (CC 76) — REFERENCE VOL  static carrier volume (default loud)
#   K4 (CC 77) — SWEEPER VOL    moving voice volume (default loud)
#   K7 (CC 19) — REVERB         wet (the sweeper sounds gorgeous in space)
#
# Try this:
#   1. K1 ~0.3, K2 ~0.2 — slow ±600 Hz sweep, easy to track by ear.
#   2. Crank K2 up. As the sweep speeds, the zero-crossing becomes a
#      "scrub" through silence — like a tape head passing the splice.
#   3. K1 to max, K2 mid. Wide sweep, full transit. The sweeper goes
#      from a high whistle, slides down through zero, comes back as a
#      bass note, returns. Reference stays put as a tonal anchor.

let sweepDepth = midi_cc(74)        # 0 → ±2000 Hz
let sweepRate  = midi_cc(71)        # slow → fast LFO
let refVol     = midi_cc(76)        # static carrier
let sweepVol   = midi_cc(77)        # moving voice
let revAmt     = midi_cc(19)

let CARRIER = 440.0

# ===== REFERENCE — static 440 Hz carrier =====
# Pure phasor_pair, real component, no further processing. The "before"
# tone the ear uses as a reference while the sweeper passes through zero.
play reference:
  let p = phasor_pair(CARRIER)
  let s = p[0] * 0.18 * refVol
  [s, s]

# ===== SWEEPER — heterodyne-shifted via SSB cmul =====
# Modulator rate is a slow sinusoidal LFO from -depth to +depth Hz.
# The audible frequency is CARRIER + modRate, which sweeps across zero
# whenever |modRate| approaches CARRIER. At the zero crossing the
# tone IS silence (DC); on the other side it re-emerges as a pitched
# tone whose audible frequency is |CARRIER + modRate|.
#
# Because the inputs are both phasor_pairs, the lower sideband is
# suppressed by construction. There is no "ghost" frequency at
# CARRIER - modRate to confuse the ear — the sweep is the only thing
# you hear besides the reference.
play sweeper:
  let depth = sweepDepth * 2000.0
  let rate = 0.033 + sweepRate * 0.30   # 1 cycle per 30s → per ~3s
  let lfo = sin(TAU * phasor(rate))     # -1 → +1
  let modRate = lfo * depth             # ±depth Hz, smoothly

  let carrier = phasor_pair(CARRIER)
  let modu = phasor_pair(modRate)
  let mixed = cmul(carrier[0], carrier[1], modu[0], modu[1])

  let s = mixed[0] * 0.18 * sweepVol
  [s, s]

# ===== MASTER =====
let dry  = reference + sweeper
let mono = (dry[0] + dry[1]) * 0.5
let wet  = mono |> reverb(4.0, 0.5)
([dry[0], dry[1]] + [wet, wet] * revAmt * 0.5) * 0.7
